  "question": {
    "text": "A bead of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;can slide without friction along a vertical ring of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. One end of a spring of force constant&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac></math>&nbsp;is connected to the bead and the other&nbsp;end is fixed at the centre of the ring. Initially, the bead is at the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>&nbsp;and due to a small push it starts sliding down the ring. If the bead momentarily loses contact with the ring at the instant when the spring makes an angle of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math>&nbsp;with the vertical, then the natural length of the spring is<br />\r<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ci/image9-110c4690-0d8a-43c5-886a-45aee07daa51.jpg\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 115px;\" />",
    "image": null
  },
